config PINEAPPLE_CORE
    bool "Pineapple OS kernel core"
    default n
    help
      Enable the Pineapple OS kernel identity and system metadata interface
      (/sys/kernel/pineapple/). Built into the kernel when selected by the
      Pineapple config.

config PINEAPPLE_BSD
    bool "Pineapple BSD compatibility layer"
    default n
    help
      FreeBSD-inspired sysctl/kinfo interface for the Pineapple OS hybrid
      kernel (Linux core + BSD-style userland APIs, like XNU = Mach + BSD).
      Exposes /proc/sys/pineapple/{ostype,osrelease,osversion} and
      /sys/kernel/pineapple/bsd/.

config PINEAPPLE_BOOT
    bool "Pineapple boot-args (PowerBook ID + PNFS)"
    default n
    help
      Parses -pineapplepowerbookid= and -pineapplefs= from the kernel command
      line inside the KERNEL (not only initramfs) and exports them via
      /sys/kernel/pineapple/boot/.

config PINEAPPLE_BOOT_ENFORCE
    bool "Panic without a valid PowerBook ID (kernel-level)"
    depends on PINEAPPLE_BOOT
    default n
    help
      Kernel-level enforcement: without a valid -pineapplepowerbookid=<ID>
      (checked against the list baked into THIS build from ids.txt), the
      kernel panics macOS-style before userspace runs. Defense in depth on
      top of the initramfs check.

config PINEAPPLE_HWCHECK
    bool "Hardware allowlist check (per build)"
    default n
    help
      Bakes Kernel/hwlist.txt into THIS build. At boot, any PCI display or
      network device not on the list triggers a macOS-style kernel panic.
      Each build has its own allowlist — hardware accepted depends on which
      cards were chosen when compiling that kernel.

config PINEAPPLE_PNFS
    bool "PNFS — Pineapple Native File System (kernel)"
    default n
    help
      In-kernel registration of the PNFS filesystem ("mount -t pnfs").
      First kernel stage of the Pineapple Native File System; the userspace
      metadata module remains .bfsprivate.
